  10: # description:
  11: # In the input you will see one or two medium-sized multicolor objects, and some multicolor pixels sprinkled around in one or two clusters
  12: # To make the output, take each of the medium-sized object and move it so that it perfectly covers some of the pixels sprinkled around, matching colors wherever they overlap.
  13: # You can rotate and flip the objects in order to make them fit the pixels, but they have to match colors wherever they overlap.
  14: 
  15: def main(input_grid):
  16:     """
  17:     Place each large multicolor object over the sparse matching pixels after trying rotations and flips.
  18: 
  19:     Input structure:
  20:     - The input contains one or two larger multicolor objects on a black background.
  21:     - It also contains small clusters of colored pixels that are partial views of those objects.
  22:     - Objects may need rotation or reflection to align with their pixel guides.
  23: 
  24:     Rule:
  25:     - Separate large objects from small pixel clusters.
  26:     - Generate rotated and flipped variants of each large object.
  27:     - Choose the placement and variant that covers the most guide pixels without any color mismatch.
  28: 
  29:     Key insight:
  30:     - The sparse pixels are anchors for transformed copies: color-consistent overlap identifies both
  31:       the orientation and the position of each object.
  32:     """
  33:     # Plan:
  34:     # 1. Break the input into 1-2 big object(s) and a mask containing the remaining pixels
  35:     # 2. For each object, create rotations and flips of it
  36:     # 3. For each object, find the best way to place one of its rotations/flips so that it covers the most number of the pixels (remember the pixel has to match object color)
  37: 
  38:     # Extract the objects from the input and categorize them into objects and pixels
  39:     # Objects can be multicolored
  40:     connected_components = find_components(input_grid, bg=Color.BLACK, connectivity=4, monochromatic=False, return_format="objects")
  41:     objects = [ cc for cc in connected_components if np.count_nonzero(cc != Color.BLACK) > 4 ]
  42:     pixels = [ cc for cc in connected_components if np.count_nonzero(cc != Color.BLACK) <= 4 ]
  43: 
  44:     # Make the pixel mask, which shows where the pixels are. These guide the placement of objects.
  45:     pixel_mask = np.full(input_grid.shape, Color.BLACK)
  46:     for pixel_object in pixels:
  47:         paste(pixel_mask, pixel_object, 0, 0, transparent=Color.BLACK, clip=True)
  48:     
  49:     output_grid = np.full(input_grid.shape, Color.BLACK)
  50:     
  51:     # For each object, find the best way to place it so that it covers the most number of the pixels
  52:     for obj in objects:
  53:         # The object can be rotated and flipped to match the pixels
  54:         # First, convert it to a sprite before transforming, because these operations are independent of position
  55:         _b = bbox(obj != Color.BLACK)
